    "body": "### Problem\n\n## The problem\n\nWhen creating a new line with `autoindent` enabled, the new line automatically indents to the indent level of your code. However, this indentation is undone when either a) changing lines or b) entering normal mode. This feels like unexpected behaviour: I expect to be able to perform normal mode operations on my new lines while keeping the indent.\n\n## Vim issue\n\nvim/vim#7941\n\nThis issue was closed with a workaround. You replace `o` in normal mode and `<CR>` in insert mode so that a character is inserted then deleted, or the cursor is moved left and right. Example configuration:\n\n```\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'o', 'o.<BS>', { remap=false })\n-- I couldn't get <CR> in insert mode working with vim.keymap.set\nvim.cmd([[inoremap <CR> <CR>.<BS>]])\n```\n\nWhere the dot is any character that can be inserted then deleted.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nI expect to be able to change into normal mode after creating a new line without undoing an autoindent. I can meet this expectation with the workaround, but I would like the underlying logic to be changed so that autoindents don't undo when changing into normal mode.\n\nI think we also need a discussion about what the undo of the autoindent is even for, that wasn't answered in vim/vim#7941.",
